# Worktree Dev Environments

Per-session isolated Odoo envs. Each pipeline run (one task, or a block of tasks)
is keyed by a `<slug>` and gets its own worktree + port + DB + data_dir + systemd unit. Many can run in parallel. This
is the only place Odoo runs in development: there is no main Odoo, no reverse
proxy and no main database, only the shared postgres cluster
(`postgres{{ service_suffix }}.service`, :{{ postgres_port }}) that holds every session's DB.

| | Session `<slug>` |
|---|---|
| Code | `.worktrees/<slug>` (branch off `origin/{{ odoo_version }}` in `src/{{ custom_repo_name }}`) |
| Runtime | `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/{odoo.conf,data,port,db,dump}` |
| Service | `odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service` |
| DB | `wt_<slug>_<MMDDYY>`, written `<DB>` below: its own restore of the prod dump, `<MMDDYY>` the dump's day from its name (`odoo_2026-10-20-18-30-06.dump` -> `wt_kio_1234_cash_in_cogs_102026`), recorded in `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/db`. An env restored before dated names keeps `wt_<slug>` |
| Env repo | `.worktrees/_envrepo/<slug>`, only with `--env-repo`: this project's own repo on branch `<slug>`, off origin's default branch |
| HTTP | `http://<DB>.localhost:<PORT>`, port auto-allocated in **{{ (odoo_major | int + 11) * 100 }}–{{ (odoo_major | int + 11) * 100 + 99 }}** (direct, no nginx) |
| Browser | `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/browser/`: what `wt-browser.sh` writes (page snapshots, console logs, saved logins, demo files) |

## Commands

Invoke by absolute path under `${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/`:

- **`wt-start.sh <slug> [--env-repo]`** — bring the env up: add the worktree off `origin/{{ odoo_version }}` as
  branch `<slug>`, allocate a free port, build the session addons farm, restore
  `<DB>` from the prod seed dump, the newest in `backup/` or the one `WT_DUMP=<path>` names (+ `odoo neutralize`, an update of the custom
  modules changed since the dump, dev fixups), start
  `odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service`.
  **Idempotent — re-run to refresh** (e.g. after adding a new module dir). Prints
  `WORKTREE:` / `BRANCH:` / `URL:` / `CONF:` / `DB:` / `PORT:` — capture these.
  **`--env-repo`** also checks out this project's own repo at `.worktrees/_envrepo/<slug>`
  on branch `<slug>` (an existing local branch of that name as it is, else a new one off
  origin's default branch, freshly fetched), for what the task changes outside the addons:
  a ticket script under `scripts/<TICKET>/`, an ADR, a skill. Commit and push from there, and
  open that PR from there: the base checkout stays on its branch for every other session.
  Use it when the task needs it; a later run without the flag keeps the one that exists, and
  every run prints `ENVREPO:` while it does. The flag needs the project to be a git clone
  with an `origin` (exit 1 before anything is made); an unknown option or a second slug is
  refused with exit 2.
  Refuses to run from a Claude worktree of the project (`.claude/worktrees/<name>`,
  exit 4, before anything is created): the desktop app's worktree guard refuses
  Edit/Write in the base checkout, which is where `.worktrees/<slug>` lives, so the
  session would get an env it cannot edit. Addons work runs in a session started in
  the base checkout (`use_worktree: false`); `WT_ALLOW_CLAUDE_WORKTREE=1` lets a
  read-only or test-only run through.
- **`wt-stop.sh <slug>`** — wait for a `wt-start.sh` or `wt-restore.sh` building the env,
  then for a `wt-test.sh` run on it (see the gotchas), stop the env's Odoo; if the worktree is clean, remove it,
  drop `<DB>`, delete the runtime dir (the branch stays on origin). A **dirty**
  worktree is kept (worktree + env + DB) and reported so no work is lost. The env-repo
  worktree counts the same: removed with the env when clean (its branch stays), and
  while it is dirty everything is kept. Exit 1 when
  it cannot take the env's start or test lock (`start.lock` or `test.lock` not a writable
  file): env and service left as they are.
- **`wt-list.sh`** — table of all active envs: slug / port / DB / service, then the
  addons worktree and the env-repo worktree, each `clean`, `dirty` or `none`.
- `wt-bootstrap.sh` — one-time, light: installs the `odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@.service` template unit
  and checks the seed dump is present. **No DB work.**
  `wt-start.sh` calls it automatically.
- **`wt-test.sh <slug> <odoo args...>`** — a one-shot run against the session, tests
  above all (`wt-test.sh <slug> -u <module> --test-enable --test-tags /<module>`):
  the session conf and DB, a free port, the IPv4 loopback, a dbfilter naming the DB.
  Refuses a `-u` of a module the DB does not have installed (exit 2, before Odoo
  starts: a seed dump older than the module, use `-i`) and fails a `--test-enable` /
  `--test-tags` run whose summary reports 0 tests (exit 3: a selector without the
  leading `/`, say). `--test-file` alone is not counted (Odoo prints no summary for
  it); a run whose output has no summary (a `--logfile` of your own) keeps Odoo's
  exit code and warns. It reads its arguments as Odoo does (optparse over the options
  `odoo server --help` lists): a repeated `-u` or `-i` keeps its last value, an option's
  value is no option (`--test-tags -utm` updates nothing), abbreviations and Odoo 19's `-t`
  count. Arguments Odoo would refuse skip both checks with a WARNING and go to Odoo as
  given. Runs on one env take turns: a second run waits for the first to end, then runs
  (see the gotchas). TERM or HUP to a test run is passed on to Odoo (its clean stop); a
  terminal's Ctrl-C reaches Odoo itself; whatever Odoo got, it is sent a TERM 30 s later if
  it still runs, and the script exits 128 + the signal.
- **`wt-browser.sh <slug> [--as <label>] <playwright-cli command...>`** — the env's own
  browser: `playwright-cli` driving the system's Chrome, headless, held to the env's URL.
  See [The session browser](#the-session-browser).
- `wt-link.sh <tree> <farm>` / `wt-restore.sh <slug>` — internals used by `wt-start`.

**Slug** = kebab branch name, conventionally `<kio-key-lower>-<short-desc>`
(e.g. `kio-1234-cash-in-cogs`). It is the git branch, the systemd instance, and (with
`-`→`_`, prefixed `wt_`, the dump's `_<MMDDYY>` after it) the DB name.

Standalone: "spin up an env for {{ ticket_prefix }}-1234" → `wt-start.sh kio-1234-<desc>`, hand back
the `URL`. "what envs are running" → `wt-list.sh`. "tear down kio-1234" →
`wt-stop.sh kio-1234-<desc>`.

## Prerequisite — the seed dump

Each session restores `backup/*.dump` (a raw prod dump) into its own DB.
{% if backup_s3_bucket %}
Fetch / refresh it with **`nix run .#download-backup`** — that app `aws s3 cp`s the
latest prod dump into `backup/` and stops there (no `odoo.conf` in the project, so no
restore of its own). Sessions already running keep their DBs; the next `wt-start`
of a new slug restores the new dump. If `wt-start` reports no dump, ask the user to
run it once first.
{% else %}
Place a prod dump into `backup/` manually (any `*.dump` produced by `pg_dump -Fc`);
the newest file is used. If `wt-start` reports no dump, ask the user to drop one in.
{% endif %}

**Check the dump's name before a restore: it is the only reliable date.** A dump named
`<db>_YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS.dump` carries its day (the dump host's clock), and a new env's DB is named
after it: `wt_<slug>_<MMDDYY>`. `wt-start` prints the dump in its `seed dump:` STATUS line
and the name in its `DB:` line; check the date against the state you need (a record created
or validated before or after that moment). `backup/` is shared: whoever refreshes it changes
the dump every new env restores. For a specific dump, keep it outside `backup/` and name it
with `WT_DUMP`:

{% if backup_s3_bucket %}
```bash
aws s3 cp s3://{{ backup_s3_bucket }}/<db>_<YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS>.dump <a folder outside backup/>
WT_DUMP=<that file> bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-start.sh <slug>
```
{% else %}
```bash
WT_DUMP=<a dump outside backup/> bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-start.sh <slug>
```
{% endif %}

`WT_DUMP` and the date only matter when a DB is restored: a re-run on an env whose DB
exists reuses it. `wt-start` records the dump it named the DB after
(`.worktrees/_env/<slug>/dump`), so `wt-restore` takes that file even if `backup/` changes
meanwhile.

## The session browser

`wt-browser.sh` is how a session env's UI is driven: UI validation, the phone check, a
`dev` agent's look at its own change, the demo video. It needs no visible pane.
Production, tickets and other sites stay with the built-in browser and the Odoo connector.

```bash
B="${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-browser.sh"
bash "$B" <slug> open                                   # Chrome, headless, at <URL>/web
bash "$B" <slug> fill "input[name=login]" admin
bash "$B" <slug> fill "input[name=password]" admin --submit
bash "$B" <slug> snapshot                               # the page as a tree; refs (e15) are what click and fill take
bash "$B" <slug> find "Confirm"                         # only the nodes that match
bash "$B" <slug> click e15
bash "$B" <slug> console error                          # JS errors since the page loaded
bash "$B" <slug> --raw eval "document.title"            # --raw: the value alone
bash "$B" <slug> close
```

- **Commands** are `playwright-cli`'s, passed as given: `playwright-cli --help`, and
  `playwright-cli --help <command>`. A target is a snapshot ref, a CSS selector or a
  Playwright locator (`"getByRole('button', { name: 'Confirm' })"`). `open`, `goto` and
  `tab-new` take a path (`/web`) as a URL on this env. A `goto` that changes only the
  `#hash` does not route the web client: click through the menus, or change the query
  (`/web?r=1#...`).
- **Output paths are relative to `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/browser/`**, the script's cwd:
  a snapshot is `.playwright-cli/page-<time>.yml` there, a `screenshot --filename=x.png`
  lands there. The folder goes with the env (`wt-stop.sh` also closes its sessions).
- **Held to the env.** A URL argument on another host is refused (exit 2); the allowed
  hosts are `<DB>.localhost:<PORT>` and `<DB>.<label>.localhost:<PORT>`. The browser itself
  requests nothing else either (the CLI's `allowedOrigins`): fonts, analytics and other
  outside resources of the prod copy's pages show in `console` as
  `net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT` and are no defect, nor is a `500` on `/web/image/...`
  (attachments are not seeded). Neither check is a sandbox: code given to `run-code`
  navigates where it likes. `attach`, `install-browser`, `kill-all`, `-s` and `open`'s
  `--browser`, `--config`, `--headed` are refused.
- **Sessions.** A session is one browser with its own cookies: `<slug>` by default,
  `<slug>-<label>` with `--as <label>` (a second user, the phone). `open` on a session that
  is open keeps it, login included, and only goes to the URL given; `close` ends it,
  `close-all` every session of the env. A headless session shuts down after an hour
  without commands: `open` again and log in again. `list` shows the env's sessions.
- **Phone.** `--as phone open --mobile`: 360x732, touch, a mobile user agent, the web
  client's phone layout.
- **One login for several sessions.** `state-save <name>.json` in a logged-in session,
  `state-load <name>.json` then `goto /web` in another: the second session is that user
  without typing the password again.
- **As a real user.** A UI test case or a demo runs as a user who holds the role, not as
  admin: set a throwaway password on that user in the session DB (below, "Several users
  side by side"), log in through `fill`, and keep the password out of every file, ticket,
  message, kb record and PR. The next restore resets it.
- **Exit 5 means the tool cannot run here**: `playwright-cli` or Chrome
  (`/opt/google/chrome/chrome`) is missing, or the browser did not start (a Chrome newer
  than the pinned playwright-core drives). The message says which. UI validation then
  goes to the built-in browser; a demo is skipped. `open` exits with `playwright-cli`'s
  own code when the browser started and the page did not load (the env's Odoo is down).
- **Versions.** `playwright-cli` is pinned in `nix/playwright-cli/package-lock.json` and
  built by the flake into the dev profile with its node, Playwright's recording ffmpeg and
  an ffmpeg for the mp4; Chrome is the system's. A test or production profile has none of
  it. A new pin comes through the template.

### Recording a demo

`wt-browser.sh <slug> [--as <label>] record <scenario.js> <KEY> [--append]` runs a
scenario in the session, which is open and logged in, and writes
`.worktrees/_env/<slug>/browser/<KEY>_demo.mp4`: H.264, 1280x720, 25 fps. It prints
`VIDEO:`, `DURATION:`, `SIZE:` and `SHEET:` (`<KEY>_demo.png`, nine frames across the
video in one image: Read it to check the take), and a `WARNING:` past 90 s.

- A scenario is one function `async (page, demo) => { ... }`; `scripts/demo-scenario.js`
  is the reference, `scripts/demo-helpers.js` the `demo` it gets: `demo.url`,
  `demo.start()`, `demo.title(title, role)` (the title card), `demo.caption(text)`,
  `demo.callout(locator, text)`, `demo.step(text, action)`. `page` is Playwright's
  ([`page.screencast`](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-screencast) draws the overlays).
- The scenario never logs in and holds no password: the session it runs in is logged in.
- `--append` adds the recording to the end of the `<KEY>_demo.mp4` already there. A flow
  across two users records each part in that user's session:
  `record part1.js <KEY>`, then `--as <label> record part2.js <KEY> --append`.
- A scenario that throws, or records nothing, exits 1 with the error; the scenario as it
  ran is `<KEY>_demo.run.js`, whose lines the error names. Exit 5 here also covers a
  missing ffmpeg.

## How it works / gotchas

- **Why a per-session data_dir, not just `addons_path`:** Odoo's `initialize_sys_path`
  appends `data_dir/addons/{{ odoo_version }}` **before** `addons_path`, and `get_module_path`
  takes the first hit — so the main symlink farm would always shadow a worktree on
  `addons_path`. Each session therefore has its own data_dir whose farm IS the
  source of truth: custom modules → the worktree, OCA/CE → the shared pinned `src/`.
- **Why sessions restore the dump themselves:** the seed flow targets a single
  DB; `wt-restore.sh` restores the already-fetched dump into `<DB>_restoring`,
  neutralizes it and only then renames it to `<DB>`, so parallel sessions stay
  independent and a running session service never sees the raw copy.
- **The dump is raw prod** → `wt-restore.sh` runs Odoo's native neutralization
  (`odoo neutralize` — every installed module's `data/neutralize.sql`: outgoing mail
  blocked by a dummy SMTP server, fetchmail off, crons off except autovacuum,
  payment/IAP disabled, `database.is_neutralized` flag +
  visible banner), then the shared `nix/scripts/dev-fixup.sql` (admin → `admin`/`admin`,
  requeue stuck queue jobs, bump the document sequences past the names the dump
  already carries — imported picking/MO names sit above the sequence, so the Nth
  create would collide on `stock_picking_name_uniq`).
- **A dump older than the code:** a module that gained a field after the dump was
  taken makes the registry load fail on the missing column (`column res_users.<field>
  does not exist`). So between `odoo neutralize` and the dev fixups, `wt-restore.sh`
  runs one `odoo -u` on the staging DB of the custom modules (dirs with a
  `__manifest__.py` in the session worktree) whose files differ between the worktree,
  uncommitted changes included, and the last `origin/{{ odoo_version }}` commit before the dump's
  day, restricted to the modules installed in the dump. The day comes from the name
  `<db>_YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS.dump` and starts at its earliest instant anywhere
  (UTC+14), whatever the dump host's zone; a dump named otherwise updates every
  installed custom module and says so in a `WARNING:`. The base is mainline's, never
  the session branch's: a kept branch's own commits are not in the dump, whatever
  their date. A kept branch behind mainline gets the modules mainline changed since
  its fork point updated with its older code: rebase it before `wt-start`. A dependency a changed manifest gained is installed by that `-u`
  itself, and then gets its own `data/neutralize.sql` (Odoo's
  `get_neutralization_queries` for exactly the modules the update installed, through
  `odoo shell`), which `odoo neutralize` ran too early to see. Only those modules'
  own queries run: a cron or mail server the new module's data creates stays active,
  as base's deactivation of both ran before it existed (its crons never fire, sessions
  run `max_cron_threads = 0`, but such a mail server could send). The attachments the update writes move with the DB to its final name. The `STATUS:`
  line names the modules; with nothing changed there is no update. Any failure drops
  the copy and fails the start, so `<DB>` never exists half-updated: read
  `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/update.log`. A reused DB (re-run of `wt-start`) is not
  updated: `-u` it with `wt-test.sh`.
- **What that update misses:** it sees only custom modules whose files changed since
  the last mainline commit before the dump's day. So (a) an `addons.yaml` pin bump
  since the dump (an OCA repo, or Odoo itself) is not updated, and (b) a module merged
  before the dump's day but deployed to prod after it is still old in the dump and not
  in the list. Update those by hand on the session:
  `systemctl --user stop odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service`, then
  `wt-test.sh <slug> -u <module>[,<module>...]` (for an Odoo pin bump, `-u all`), then
  `systemctl --user start odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service`. A dependency such a
  hand `-u` installs gets no neutralization of its own (only `wt-start`'s update does
  that): check its crons and mail servers.
- **Concurrency:** port allocation + `git worktree add` run under `flock`
  (`.worktrees/.wt.lock`); the slow `pg_restore` runs outside the lock so parallel
  starts overlap. DBs/ports/worktrees are per-slug, so sessions never collide. Per env,
  `wt-start.sh` holds `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/start.lock` for its whole run, and
  `wt-restore.sh` takes it too: a second `wt-start` of the slug waits for the first, and
  `wt-stop.sh` waits before it drops anything (a stop mid-restore used to drop
  `<DB>_restoring` and the env dir under it).
- **Runtime:** sessions run `workers = 0`, `max_cron_threads = 0`. In this threaded
  mode Odoo serves the bus websocket (`/websocket`) on the session's own HTTP port,
  so live updates, chat and notifications work with no nginx and no evented port;
  only the prod layout (workers + evented port behind a proxy) cannot be reproduced
  here. The `queue_job` runner still starts in threaded mode against the session
  DB — jobs process normally and isolated. It calls the session back at
  `http://<DB>.localhost:<PORT>/queue_job/runjob` (the conf's `[queue_job]`
  section): left to `http_interface` its host would be `::1`, an invalid URL host,
  and `^%d$` passes only a Host carrying the DB name. Channels: `root:3`; a
  sub-channel the conf does not name runs in its parent.
- **The DB name is the subdomain:** sessions run `dbfilter = ^%d$`, so `<URL>` is
  `http://<DB>.localhost:<PORT>` and bare `localhost:<PORT>` lands on the DB
  selector. The point is the browser: cookies are per host, not per port, so two
  sessions opened on bare `localhost` would share one `session_id` and log each
  other out. RPC with an explicit database ignores dbfilter and works on any host.
  `*.localhost` resolves to `::1`, so sessions listen on the IPv6 loopback only
  (`http_interface = ::1`): reachable from this machine, never from the LAN (the
  session DB is a prod copy with `admin`/`admin`); a client that asks for
  `127.0.0.1` gets a refused connection. `wt-start.sh` pins `report.url` (what
  wkhtmltopdf resolves report assets against) to `<URL>`'s host.
- **Several users side by side in one browser — add a label after the DB name:**
  `%d` is only the host's *first* label, while the `session_id` cookie is host-only
  (keyed on the full host). So `http://<DB>.<user>.localhost:<PORT>` opens the
  same DB with its own login, next to `<URL>`: e.g. admin on `<DB>.localhost`
  and the reporting user on `<DB>.<user>.localhost`. No conf change, no hosts
  entry (`*.localhost` at any depth is loopback). This is the way to reproduce a
  "user X can't do Y" report in a browser with no containers. To log in as a real
  user, set a throwaway password on that user in the session DB (`odoo shell`:
  `env['res.users'].browse(<uid>).password = '...'`, commit); the next restore
  resets it. Limit: `report.url` stays pinned to `<URL>`, so wkhtmltopdf fetches
  report assets with the main host's session.
- **On a shared test server — `WT_*` lines in the project `.env`:** the defaults
  above are a developer machine. A server whose postgres is another host, and whose
  sessions are browsed from the LAN, sets (no quotes; `wt-common.sh` reads only these
  five keys from `.env`):

  ```
  WT_DB_HOST=<postgres host>        # default localhost
  WT_DB_PORT=<postgres port>        # default {{ postgres_port }}
  WT_DB_PASSWORD=<role password>    # default the answers' password
  WT_HTTP_INTERFACE=0.0.0.0         # default ::1 (loopback only)
  WT_HOST_SUFFIX=<box>.<domain>     # default localhost
  ```

  `<URL>` becomes `http://<DB>.<WT_HOST_SUFFIX>:<PORT>` (also `report.url` and
  the `[queue_job]` host), so `WT_HOST_SUFFIX` needs a **wildcard** DNS record to the
  box (`*.<box>.<domain>` at any depth, as `*.localhost` is locally); the extra-label
  trick then reads `<DB>.<user>.<box>.<domain>`. `WT_HTTP_INTERFACE=0.0.0.0`
  serves the neutralized prod copy (`admin`/`admin`) to that LAN — an explicit
  opt-in, for a server whose main Odoo already is a LAN test copy. An interface other
  than loopback (`::1`, `localhost`, `127.0.0.0/8`) also gets `list_db = False` and a
  random `admin_passwd`, new on every `wt-start`: the database manager
  (`/web/database`: back up, restore, drop any DB on that postgres) is off for the LAN.
  A loopback session keeps `admin_passwd = odoo` with the manager on. The conf is mode
  0600 either way (it carries `db_password`). A role that is not
  a superuser works: session DBs are dropped through `drop_db`, which falls back
  from `WITH (FORCE)` when an autovacuum worker is on the DB.
- **Run tests through `wt-test.sh`, never `odoo -c <CONF>` by hand.** A bare run
  breaks three ways: it takes the session's port, so a browser tab still open on
  `<URL>` (logged in, polling its systray and bus) sends its requests into the
  running tests' transaction and they die on state they never set; it listens on
  `::1` only, while HttpCase talks to `127.0.0.1`; and `^%d$` maps the Host
  `127.0.0.1` to no database. `wt-test.sh` takes a free port, listens on
  `127.0.0.1` (still loopback only) and passes `--db-filter ^<DB>$`.
- **Test runs on one env wait for each other, and stop and restore wait for them.** Two runs on one DB collide: the second
  died at registry load on a lock timeout (`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ...`) while the first's
  `-u` held the tables. `wt-test.sh` holds an exclusive `flock` on
  `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/test.lock` for the whole Odoo run, so a second run prints
  that it is waiting and on whom (pid and command), blocks until the first ends, then
  runs. A run that seems stuck before Odoo starts is waiting there. `wt-stop.sh` takes
  the same lock (its waiting line ends `then stopping the env`) before it stops the
  session's service, decides whether the worktree is dirty and tears anything down; an
  env removed and re-created while it waited has its new lock waited for in turn. On
  every restore that does not reuse the DB, `wt-restore.sh` holds the lock while it swaps
  the fresh copy in as `<DB>` (filestore move, drop of the old DB, rename): neither
  drops a DB under a running test. `wt-start.sh` holds it to write `report.url` and
  restart the session's service (its waiting line ends `then restarting the session
  service`), so a re-run never puts the service on the DB beside a running `-u`. A run still waiting when `wt-stop.sh` removed the env exits 1
  (`env <slug> was stopped while this run waited`) rather than run against nothing. A command of your
  own other than `wt-test.sh` takes its turn with
  `flock "${{ project_dir_var }}/.worktrees/_env/<slug>/test.lock" <command>`; `wt-test.sh` needs no
  wrapper (wrapped anyway, it finds its caller holding the lock and runs under it).
- **Reading or checking a session from Claude:** `{{ odoo_cmd }} shell -c <CONF> --no-http`
  with a script on stdin (as a given user: `env = env(user=env.ref('base.user_admin'))`
  or `record.with_user(user)`), or `psql -d <DB>` for plain reads; its UI through
  `wt-browser.sh`. There is no local Odoo MCP; the Odoo connector is production, as
  your own account.
- **The env-repo worktree is for editing and committing, nothing else.** Its `src/`
  is empty (`src/*/` is gitignored) and it has no env of its own, so a tool run from its
  own copy of `.claude/skills/` (find-code's index, `dbfields.py`, the `wt-*.sh`) sees no
  code or the wrong project. Run tools from the base checkout
  (`${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/...`); `dbfields.py` and the env band read a cwd
  in `.worktrees/_envrepo/<slug>/` as the env `<slug>`.
- Cosmetic: every session shows Odoo's native neutralization banner (same on all) —
  tell sessions apart by the subdomain in `<URL>`.
- Disk: each session DB is a full restore (a few GB); attachments are not seeded
  (the dump carries the database, not the filestore). Plenty of headroom, but 5
  parallel ≈ a handful of restores running at once.

## Files

`.worktrees/<slug>/` (worktree), `.worktrees/_env/<slug>/` (conf, data_dir, port) and
`.worktrees/_envrepo/<slug>/` (env-repo worktree) are gitignored in the parent repo. The
addons branch lives in the nested addons repo (`src/{{ custom_repo_name }}`, remote
`{{ module_prefix }}-dev/{{ custom_repo_name }}`), the env-repo branch in the parent repo itself.
